WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.980
Summer 2017 was chock-full of great films,
from blockbuster superhero sagas to streaming-only

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:06.500
surprises.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:11.900
Franchises were revived with vigor … franchises
were brilliantly wrapped up ... legacies were

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.680
secured, and Charlize Theron probably had
more fun than anyone in the business.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.360
These are the best of the best from a season
full of fantastic films.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:20.800
Wonder Woman

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.040
After 75-odd years of whooping bad guy butt
in the comics pages, Wonder Woman finally

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:31.380
got her shot at solo blockbuster glory, just
in time to either make or break the emerging

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:32.800
DC Extended Universe.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:36.800
Wonder Woman did everything it needed to and
then some, serving as an effective origin

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.180
story as well as a superhero adventure that
stood on its own as a standalone experience

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.560
while continuing to lay the groundwork for
the DCEU's big team-up in Justice League.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:50.480
The reviews have been almost universally positive,
and audiences have turned out in droves, laying

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:54.850
the groundwork for a real battle for superhero
supremacy at the box office between Marvel

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:55.400
and DC.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:56.880
Atomic Blonde

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.720
This neon thriller takes place in the final
days of the Cold War: The Berlin Wall is about

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.700
to go down, but that doesn't mean the cloak-and-dagger
business is over.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:09.600
To the contrary: a super-spy played by Charlize
Theron is sent to Germany to retrieve a stolen

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.060
list that contains the names of undercover
spies.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:15.780
The list has fallen into the hands of the
Soviets, and Charlize will have to bash a

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.180
few skulls in her quest to discover that document.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.080
But honestly, the plot isn't that important.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.540
The insane fight choreography is the star
here: Charlize beats up dudes using everything

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.260
from shoes to ropes to cooking pots, all while
wearing the most stylish clothes imaginable.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:33.200
The actress did her own stunts for Atomic
Blonde, adding a touch of realism to the brutal

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.620
battle scenes and car chases.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.740
"Fasten your seatbelt."

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:41.920
Okja

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.260
Netflix's Okja is a film that starts off feeling
like a G-rated kid's adventure and ends up

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.580
inside an very R-rated slaughterhouse.

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:53.840
The film tells the story of a young girl who's
friends with a hippo-like "super pig" named

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:59.180
Okja that actually belongs to the all-powerful
Mirando Corporation, a business run by evil

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.620
twins, both played by Tilda Swinton, obviously.

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:05.700
Okja is their genetically modified creation,
and they plan on turning the poor pig into

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:09.860
the tastiest, most eco-friendly pork chops
on the planet ... but not if the girl and

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.280
a team of animal activists rescue Okja first.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.060
A darkly comic vision of future, Okja has
quite a lot to say about the evils of capitalism

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:21.799
— and the merits of a plant-based diet — while
also offering up a wildly entertaining and

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.220
inventive piece of filmmaking.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.180
War for the Planet of the Apes

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:30.090
The conclusion to one of the best film trilogies
ever, War for the Planet of the Apes expertly

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:35.160
blends the book of Exodus with Apocalypse
Now, resulting in a brutal Old Testament-P.O.W.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:35.660
story.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.820
In the fiery aftermath of the previous film,
Caesar has led his followers into the mountains,

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.480
hoping to escape the wrath of man.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.650
But Caesar knows his apes can't hide in the
hills for long, so this primate Moses plans

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.660
on leading his people to a new promised land,
where they can avoid any future conflicts.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:53.900
This sets up a Bridge on the River Kwai-style
showdown between Caesar and a mad Colonel

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.100
played by Woody Harrelson.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.440
War for the Planet of the Apes is the perfect
ending to the trilogy, featuring a show-stopping,

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.080
Oscar-worthy motion-capture performance from
Andy Serkis.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:03.900
Spider-Man: Homecoming

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:08.060
One of the most charming and entertaining
films to ever swing its way into the Marvel

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:12.410
Cinematic Universe, Spider-Man: Homecoming
follows wannabe Avenger Peter Parker as he

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.360
tries to fight crime and navigate high school
at the same time.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.060
Things get even more complicated when an honest-to-goodness
supervillain shows up: Michael Keaton's Vulture

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:24.450
is the ultimate thief, and if Peter can bring
him to justice, it could be his ticket into

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:25.120
the Avengers.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.780
One of the best comic book movies ever made,
Homecoming even won over Marvel haters thanks

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.640
to its John Hughes-like high school vibe.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.019
And all that upbeat energy was largely thanks
to Tom Holland, who's a pitch-perfect Peter

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:36.520
Parker.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.340
"You can't tell anybody about this.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:38.840
You gotta keep it a secret."

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:40.080
"Secret?

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:40.580
Why?"

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.800
"Because you know what she's like, if she
finds out people try and kill me every single

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:44.300
night she's not gonna let me do this anymore!"

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:45.680
Dunkirk

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.820
Without a doubt, the epic World War II thriller
Dunkirk is one of Christopher Nolan's greatest

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.320
achievements, right up there with Inception
and The Dark Knight.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.680
Shot mostly with 65 millimeter film on IMAX
cameras, the nail-biting Dunkirk tells the

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.600
story of a real-life military retreat in 1940.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.120
Roughly 400,000 Allied troops were trapped
on a French beach, completely surrounded,

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.900
and the only thing keeping them from home
was the English Channel.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:13.700
Unfortunately, the beach was too shallow to
accommodate military-sized vessels, so English

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:17.500
civilians sprang into action, sailing to Dunkirk
in their yachts and fishing boats.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:22.160
Nolan masterfully uses three interlocking
tales to put audiences right in the middle

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:22.660
of the battlefield.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:24.360
Baby Driver

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:28.960
With its eclectic soundtrack and insanely
impressive driving stunts, Edgar Wright's

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.860
Baby Driver is one of the most exciting films
of 2017.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:36.990
This souped-up action flick stars Ansel Elgort
as Baby, a young man who works as a getaway

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:40.100
driver for a smarmy crime boss played by Kevin
Spacey.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:44.380
Suffering from tinnitus, Baby drowns out the
constant droning with an incredible collection

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:46.840
of tunes to soundtrack his incredible driving
skills.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.780
Baby wants to go straight, but he gets pulled
into a robbery-gone-wrong, so he's forced

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:53.319
to take a stand to save everything he loves.

00:04:53.319 --> 00:04:57.449
With its dance-like action and its super cool
characters, Wright's sixth feature film is

00:04:57.449 --> 00:05:01.389
like a mashup between Singin' in the Rain
and Walter Hill's The Driver, which means

00:05:01.389 --> 00:05:02.680
it's got a little something for everyone.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:04.700
"I want us to head west and never stop.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:05.800
You in?

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:06.900
I'm in, Baby."

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.120
Thanks for watching!

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:10.700
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:12.770
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

